(Program generator: emits random valid Vox source text.

 There is no AST - rules append rendered source directly into gen_out.
 Names are v1..vN in declaration order, so every reference is in scope
 by construction. Buffers (bN), lists (lN), thing instances (iN),
 maps (mN), value locals (yN), floats (dN), texts (sN), function-call
 scratch locals (cN), grid loop variables (eN), While counters (uN) and
 Repeat counters (rN) each get their own counter and are always
 self-contained within the one statement that declares them - they are
 never referenced by a later, unrelated statement, so nothing about
 Vox's per-branch declaration visibility can bite them.

 Nesting (loops, ifs) is depth-capped at 3 and threaded through two
 precomputed values on every recursive call: `depth` (nesting budget
 remaining, counted down to 0) and `indent` (the cosmetic prefix for
 this level's lines, two spaces deeper each level). A block's body is
 generated by 'gen body', which is the one place note 6b's trap is
 handled: it always ends with a plain leaf statement, so the block's
 own period reliably closes it even when the body's other action was
 itself a nested loop or if.)

Create a buffer called gen_out.
a number called gen_vars is 0.
a number called gen_bufs is 0.
a number called gen_lists is 0.
a number called gen_instances is 0.
a number called gen_args is 0.
a number called gen_maps is 0.
a number called gen_values is 0.
a number called gen_floats is 0.
a number called gen_texts is 0.
a number called gen_grid is 0.
a number called gen_whiles is 0.
a number called gen_repeats is 0.
a number called gen_envs is 0.

To 'gen emit' with a text called s.
    a buffer called piece is "{s}".
    append piece to gen_out.

To 'gen new var'.
    Set gen_vars to gen_vars add 1.
    a number called v is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a text called line is "a number called v{gen_vars} is {v}.\n".
    'gen emit' of line.
    Return a number, gen_vars.

To 'gen var ref'.
    If gen_vars is 0 then,
        Return a text, "0".
    a number called 'the pick' is 'rng below' of gen_vars.
    a number called 'the chosen variable' is 'the pick' add 1.
    a text called 'the reference' is "v{'the chosen variable'}".
    Return a text, 'the reference'.

To 'gen expr'.
    a text called 'the left operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called 'the right operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a number called 'the operator choice' is 'rng below' of 5.
    If 'the operator choice' is 0 then,
        a text called 'the sum' is "{'the left operand'} add {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the sum'.
    If 'the operator choice' is 1 then,
        a text called 'the difference' is "{'the left operand'} minus {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the difference'.
    If 'the operator choice' is 2 then,
        a text called 'the product' is "{'the left operand'} multiply {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the product'.
    If 'the operator choice' is 3 then,
        a text called 'the times product' is "{'the left operand'} times {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the times product'.
    a text called 'the plain reference' is "{'the left operand'}".
    Return a text, 'the plain reference'.

(Same four shapes as 'gen expr', minus `times` - hand-reduced: `append v1
 times v2 to l1.` fails to parse ("Expected 'to' after value in append
 statement"), while `append v1 multiply v2 to l1.` compiles, and `times`
 works everywhere else (Print, Set, declarations) including as the
 alternate spelling of `multiply` CHANGELOG 0.4.4 introduced - append's
 own value grammar is the one place that alias does not reach. Reported
 separately as a genuine finding, not tuned around; this is the
 generator-side fix so 'gen leaf butif append', the only caller that
 feeds an expression into an append statement's value, never emits the
 illegal form.)
To 'gen append expr'.
    a text called 'the left operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called 'the right operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a number called 'the operator choice' is 'rng below' of 4.
    If 'the operator choice' is 0 then,
        a text called 'the sum' is "{'the left operand'} add {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the sum'.
    If 'the operator choice' is 1 then,
        a text called 'the difference' is "{'the left operand'} minus {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the difference'.
    If 'the operator choice' is 2 then,
        a text called 'the product' is "{'the left operand'} multiply {'the right operand'}",
        Return a text, 'the product'.
    a text called 'the plain reference' is "{'the left operand'}".
    Return a text, 'the plain reference'.

(`a` is a reserved word, which is why the operands carry their full
 names.)

(A boolean condition, for the positions where one is legal - an If's own
 `then`/but-if clause, a but-if branch's own condition. Comparisons and
 property checks are grammatically confined to exactly that position in
 0.4.5 (hand-verified: `Print x is greater than y.` and even
 `a boolean called c is x is greater than y.` both fail to parse - "is
 greater than" is not a first-class expression the way `add`/`multiply`
 are, so it must never be handed to 'gen expr's callers). Covers the
 full comparison table plus `modulo ... is equal to 0` and an and/or
 join of two conditions - the operator-matrix gap this leaf-space
 exists to close.)
To 'gen condition'.
    a text called 'the left operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called 'the right operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a number called 'the comparison choice' is 'rng below' of 6.
    a text called 'the comparison' is "".
    If 'the comparison choice' is 0 then,
        Set 'the comparison' to "{'the left operand'} is greater than {'the right operand'}".
    If 'the comparison choice' is 1 then,
        Set 'the comparison' to "{'the left operand'} is less than {'the right operand'}".
    If 'the comparison choice' is 2 then,
        Set 'the comparison' to "{'the left operand'} is equal to {'the right operand'}".
    If 'the comparison choice' is 3 then,
        Set 'the comparison' to "{'the left operand'} is not equal to {'the right operand'}".
    If 'the comparison choice' is 4 then,
        Set 'the comparison' to "{'the left operand'} is greater than or equal to {'the right operand'}".
    If 'the comparison choice' is 5 then,
        Set 'the comparison' to "{'the left operand'} is less than or equal to {'the right operand'}".
    a number called 'the modulus' is 'rng below' of 5 add 2.
    a text called 'the modulo check' is "{'the left operand'} modulo {'the modulus'} is equal to 0".
    a number called 'the joiner choice' is 'rng below' of 3.
    If 'the joiner choice' is 0 then,
        Return a text, 'the comparison'.
    If 'the joiner choice' is 1 then,
        a text called 'the and form' is "{'the comparison'} and {'the modulo check'}",
        Return a text, 'the and form'.
    a text called 'the or form' is "{'the comparison'} or {'the modulo check'}".
    Return a text, 'the or form'.

(gen line piece: the punctuation a body line needs depends entirely on
 whether it is nested inside an already-open clause. Nested, every
 line but the last continues that clause's action list with a comma;
 unnested (true top level), commas do not parse at all outside a
 clause, so every line is its own period-terminated sentence. The
 comma is what keeps a name declared on one line usable on the next -
 without it, the period would close the enclosing clause immediately,
 exactly the trap note 6b describes.)
To 'gen line piece' with a text called indent and a text called content and a boolean called continues.
    If continues then,
        a text called piece is "{indent}{content},\n",
        Return a text, piece.
    a text called piece2 is "{indent}{content}.\n".
    Return a text, piece2.

To 'gen join lines' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested and a list called 'the lines'.
    a number called 'the count' is 'the lines's length.
    a number called 'the position' is 1.
    While 'the position' is less than 'the count',
        a text called 'the line' is element 'the position' of 'the lines',
        a text called piece is 'gen line piece' of indent and 'the line' and nested,
        'gen emit' of piece,
        Set 'the position' to 'the position' add 1.
    a text called 'the last line' is element 'the count' of 'the lines'.
    a text called 'final piece' is 'gen line piece' of indent and 'the last line' and false.
    'gen emit' of 'final piece'.

To 'gen leaf print' with a text called indent.
    a text called e is 'gen expr'.
    a text called line is "{indent}Print {e}.\n".
    'gen emit' of line.

To 'gen leaf assign' with a text called indent.
    a text called target is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called e is 'gen expr'.
    a text called line is "{indent}Set {target} to {e}.\n".
    'gen emit' of line.

(Buffers: declaration, a write, and a read - the language's own claim
 is that out-of-bounds access sets the error flag and returns 0 rather
 than crashing, so the oob variant is what actually puts that claim on
 trial.)
To 'gen leaf buffer inrange' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_bufs to gen_bufs add 1.
    a number called 'buf size' is 'rng below' of 64 add 8.
    a text called decl is "a buffer called b{gen_bufs} is {'buf size'} bytes in size".
    a number called pos is 'rng below' of 'buf size' add 1.
    a number called val is 'rng below' of 256.
    a text called 'write line' is "Set byte {pos} of b{gen_bufs} to {val}".
    a text called 'read line' is "Print byte {pos} of b{gen_bufs}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'write line', 'read line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen leaf buffer oob' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_bufs to gen_bufs add 1.
    a number called 'buf size' is 'rng below' of 64 add 8.
    a text called decl is "a buffer called b{gen_bufs} is {'buf size'} bytes in size".
    a number called extra is 'rng below' of 500 add 1.
    a number called oob is 'buf size' add extra.
    a text called 'read line' is "Print byte {oob} of b{gen_bufs}".
    a text called handler is "On error print \"buffer read out of range\"".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'read line', handler].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Lists: declaration, append, and element access - in range and
 deliberately out of it, same claim under test as buffers.)
To 'gen leaf list inrange' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_lists to gen_lists add 1.
    a number called 'elem one' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'elem two' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called decl is "a list called l{gen_lists} is [{'elem one'}, {'elem two'}]".
    a number called appended is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called 'append line' is "append {appended} to l{gen_lists}".
    a text called 'read line' is "Print element 1 of l{gen_lists}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'append line', 'read line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen leaf list oob' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_lists to gen_lists add 1.
    a number called 'elem one' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'elem two' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called decl is "a list called l{gen_lists} is [{'elem one'}, {'elem two'}]".
    a number called oob is 'rng below' of 500 add 10.
    a text called 'read line' is "Print element {oob} of l{gen_lists}".
    a text called handler is "On error print \"list read out of range\"".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'read line', handler].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Function calls: the generated program always defines f1/f2/f3 (0, 1,
 and 2 number parameters) in its prelude, so a call site only has to
 pick one and precompute its arguments into fresh locals - language
 note 3.)
To 'gen call zero' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called line is "Print f1".
    a list called 'the lines' is [line].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen call one' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_args to gen_args add 1.
    a number called 'arg value' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called 'arg decl' is "a number called c{gen_args} is {'arg value'}".
    a text called 'call line' is "Print f2 of c{gen_args}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['arg decl', 'call line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen call two' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_args to gen_args add 1.
    a number called 'first name' is gen_args.
    a number called 'first value' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called 'first decl' is "a number called c{'first name'} is {'first value'}".
    Set gen_args to gen_args add 1.
    a number called 'second name' is gen_args.
    a number called 'second value' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called 'second decl' is "a number called c{'second name'} is {'second value'}".
    a text called 'call line' is "Print f3 of c{'first name'} and c{'second name'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['first decl', 'second decl', 'call line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Chained expansion, the grid: 0.4.5's Cartesian-product call form, joined
 by 'and' across two 'each ... from ...' clauses - a list clause and a
 range clause, over f3's two number parameters. The loop variables get
 their own e{N} names from a dedicated counter so two grid calls in the
 same program never bind the same identifier twice, which the one
 identifier space rule would otherwise make a shadowing question rather
 than a clean fresh name. f3 has no side effect worth printing, so this
 is a bare call statement - language note 3's "plain call statement"
 form, the same one the grid section itself uses when a fixed argument
 needs to come first.)
To 'gen call grid' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_grid to gen_grid add 1.
    a number called 'first loop name' is gen_grid.
    Set gen_grid to gen_grid add 1.
    a number called 'second loop name' is gen_grid.
    a number called 'list element one' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'list element two' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'range start' is 'rng below' of 5 add 1.
    a number called 'range spread' is 'rng below' of 4.
    a number called 'range end' is 'range start' add 'range spread'.
    a text called 'call line' is "f3 of each e{'first loop name'} from [{'list element one'}, {'list element two'}] and each e{'second loop name'} from {'range start'} to {'range end'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['call line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Maps: literal creation with a text-valued key and a number-valued key,
 string-key read, `Set map's "key" to ...`, `'s length`, and a `keys`
 iteration - heap growth and hashing (finding 05). The oob variant reads
 a key that was never inserted; the language's own claim is that a
 missing key sets the error flag and returns 0 rather than crashing, so
 this is the same on-trial pattern as the buffer/list oob leaves. Each
 map gets a freshly numbered iteration variable (k{N}) for the same
 fresh-name reason the grid loop variables do.)
To 'gen leaf map inrange' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_maps to gen_maps add 1.
    a number called 'count value' is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a number called 'count value two' is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a text called decl is "a map called m{gen_maps} is {{\"label\": \"tag{gen_maps}\", \"count\": {'count value'}}}".
    a text called 'read label' is "Print m{gen_maps}'s \"label\"".
    a text called 'write count' is "Set m{gen_maps}'s \"count\" to {'count value two'}".
    a text called 'read count' is "Print m{gen_maps}'s \"count\"".
    a text called 'read length' is "Print m{gen_maps}'s length".
    a text called 'iterate keys' is "For each k{gen_maps} in m{gen_maps}'s keys, Print k{gen_maps}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'read label', 'write count', 'read count', 'read length', 'iterate keys'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen leaf map oob' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_maps to gen_maps add 1.
    a number called 'count value' is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a text called decl is "a map called m{gen_maps} is {{\"label\": \"tag{gen_maps}\", \"count\": {'count value'}}}".
    a text called 'read line' is "Print m{gen_maps}'s \"missing\"".
    a text called handler is "On error print \"map key missing\"".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'read line', handler].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(The `value` type: declare, print, reassign across the type boundary
 (number to text), print again - the exact round-trip that produced
 compiler bugs #2, #8 and #15 (finding 06). Runtime-tag ABI.)
To 'gen leaf value roundtrip' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_values to gen_values add 1.
    a number called 'starting number' is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a text called decl is "a value called y{gen_values} is {'starting number'}".
    a text called 'print number' is "Print y{gen_values}".
    a text called reassign is "Set y{gen_values} to \"reassigned{gen_values}\"".
    a text called 'print text' is "Print y{gen_values}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'print number', reassign, 'print text'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Floats: decimal literals, float multiply and divide, and a mixed
 float/int expression - the whole float codegen path, unfuzzed until
 now (finding 04). The divisor's whole part is drawn from 1 upward so it
 is never 0.0 by construction - proving the divide codegen path is the
 goal, not proving the language's own division-by-zero behaviour, which
 is a different question this generator does not ask.)
To 'gen leaf float arithmetic' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_floats to gen_floats add 1.
    a number called 'first name' is gen_floats.
    a number called 'first whole' is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a number called 'first frac' is 'rng below' of 100.
    Set gen_floats to gen_floats add 1.
    a number called 'second name' is gen_floats.
    a number called 'second whole' is 'rng below' of 20 add 1.
    a number called 'second frac' is 'rng below' of 100.
    Set gen_floats to gen_floats add 1.
    a number called 'product name' is gen_floats.
    Set gen_floats to gen_floats add 1.
    a number called 'quotient name' is gen_floats.
    Set gen_floats to gen_floats add 1.
    a number called 'mixed name' is gen_floats.
    a text called 'first decl' is "a float called d{'first name'} is {'first whole'}.{'first frac'}".
    a text called 'second decl' is "a float called d{'second name'} is {'second whole'}.{'second frac'}".
    a text called 'product decl' is "a float called d{'product name'} is d{'first name'} multiply d{'second name'}".
    a text called 'print product' is "Print d{'product name'}".
    a text called 'quotient decl' is "a float called d{'quotient name'} is d{'first name'} divide d{'second name'}".
    a text called 'print quotient' is "Print d{'quotient name'}".
    a text called 'the int operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called 'mixed decl' is "a float called d{'mixed name'} is d{'first name'} add {'the int operand'}".
    a text called 'print mixed' is "Print d{'mixed name'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['first decl', 'second decl', 'product decl', 'print product', 'quotient decl', 'print quotient', 'mixed decl', 'print mixed'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Thing member functions: the manifest entry plus its two `To do the
 T's m` definitions live in the prelude (see 'gen emit prelude thing
 methods'); this leaf drives both call forms - the type-possessive maker
 (`a t4's 'made at' with ...`) and the instance-possessive receiver
 (`i's 'reflected across'`) - and reads the owner-return field back
 after each (finding 03). Today only data fields are generated; this is
 the dispatch and owner-return path fields alone cannot reach.)
To 'gen leaf thing member' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_instances to gen_instances add 1.
    a number called 'maker name' is gen_instances.
    a number called 'starting x' is 'rng below' of 1000.
    Set gen_instances to gen_instances add 1.
    a number called 'mirror name' is gen_instances.
    a text called decl is "a t4 called i{'maker name'} is a t4's 'made at' with {'starting x'}".
    a text called 'print maker' is "Print i{'maker name'}'s x1".
    a text called 'mirror decl' is "a t4 called i{'mirror name'} is i{'maker name'}'s 'reflected across'".
    a text called 'print mirror' is "Print i{'mirror name'}'s x1".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'print maker', 'mirror decl', 'print mirror'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Mixed-type lists: a number, a text, and a float literal in one list -
 per-slot runtime type tag machinery, never author-declared. Iterating
 and printing exercises the tag dispatch on read (grep-proven gap, same
 standard as the four findings). Each list gets its own iteration
 variable (w{N}) for the same fresh-name reason maps and the grid do.)
To 'gen leaf list mixed' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_lists to gen_lists add 1.
    a number called 'number element' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'float whole' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'float frac' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called decl is "a list called l{gen_lists} is [{'number element'}, \"mixed{gen_lists}\", {'float whole'}.{'float frac'}]".
    a text called 'iterate line' is "For each w{gen_lists} in l{gen_lists}, Print w{gen_lists}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'iterate line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Text as a value the generated program handles, not just the source the
 fuzzer writes: a text local built from a literal, then a second text
 built by interpolating the first - `{...}` format substitution on the
 target program's own text data, grep-proven gap, same standard.)
To 'gen leaf text value' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_texts to gen_texts add 1.
    a number called 'first name' is gen_texts.
    Set gen_texts to gen_texts add 1.
    a number called 'second name' is gen_texts.
    a text called 'first decl' is "a text called s{'first name'} is \"word{'first name'}\"".
    a text called 'second decl' is "a text called s{'second name'} is \"{{s{'first name'}}}-{{s{'first name'}}}\"".
    a text called 'print line' is "Print s{'second name'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['first decl', 'second decl', 'print line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen leaf call' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'call kind' is 'rng below' of 4.
    If 'call kind' is 0 then,
        'gen call zero' of indent and nested.
    If 'call kind' is 1 then,
        'gen call one' of indent and nested.
    If 'call kind' is 2 then,
        'gen call two' of indent and nested.
    If 'call kind' is 3 then,
        'gen call grid' of indent and nested.

(Same three call shapes, minus the grid - kind 19's job is standing in
 for kind 6 wherever the grid's `each ... and each ...` clause would be
 unsafe as a block closer (see 'gen statement's avoid-onerror remap),
 without losing zero/one/two's coverage in that position too.)
To 'gen leaf call safe' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'call kind' is 'rng below' of 3.
    If 'call kind' is 0 then,
        'gen call zero' of indent and nested.
    If 'call kind' is 1 then,
        'gen call one' of indent and nested.
    If 'call kind' is 2 then,
        'gen call two' of indent and nested.

(Things: instance declaration, possessive field read/write, and
 whole-thing printing. t1/t2 are flat; t3 nests one of each, so a
 chained possessive (i's g1's x1) is always reachable.)
To 'gen thing flat' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_instances to gen_instances add 1.
    a number called 'type choice' is 'rng below' of 2.
    a text called 'type name' is "t1".
    If 'type choice' is 1 then,
        Set 'type name' to "t2".
    a number called val is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a text called decl is "a {'type name'} called i{gen_instances}".
    a text called 'set line' is "Set i{gen_instances}'s x1 to {val}".
    a text called 'print line' is "Print i{gen_instances}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'set line', 'print line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen thing nested' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_instances to gen_instances add 1.
    a number called val is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a text called decl is "a t3 called i{gen_instances}".
    a text called 'set line' is "Set i{gen_instances}'s g1's x1 to {val}".
    a text called 'field line' is "Print i{gen_instances}'s g1's x1".
    a text called 'whole line' is "Print i{gen_instances}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'set line', 'field line', 'whole line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen leaf thing' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'thing kind' is 'rng below' of 2.
    If 'thing kind' is 0 then,
        'gen thing flat' of indent and nested.
    If 'thing kind' is 1 then,
        'gen thing nested' of indent and nested.

(Value-copy check: assign one instance to another, mutate the copy,
 and print both fields back out - the two must differ, proving the
 copy shares nothing with the original.)
To 'gen leaf thing copy' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_instances to gen_instances add 1.
    a number called 'original name' is gen_instances.
    a number called val is 'rng below' of 1000.
    Set gen_instances to gen_instances add 1.
    a number called 'copy name' is gen_instances.
    a number called val2 is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a text called decl is "a t1 called i{'original name'}".
    a text called 'set line' is "Set i{'original name'}'s x1 to {val}".
    a text called 'copy decl' is "a t1 called i{'copy name'} is i{'original name'}".
    a text called 'mutate line' is "Set i{'copy name'}'s x1 to {val2}".
    a text called 'print orig' is "Print i{'original name'}'s x1".
    a text called 'print copy' is "Print i{'copy name'}'s x1".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'set line', 'copy decl', 'mutate line', 'print orig', 'print copy'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(`but if` chains (bugs #3 and #14 both lived here): a base action plus
 1-3 alternative branches, checked in order, first match wins. Two
 shapes, because 0.4.5's `otherwise` is hand-verified against the pinned
 binary to compile after `append`'s chain but NOT after `print`'s -
 `Print x, but if ... print y,
 otherwise print z.` is rejected ("Expected a statement, got Otherwise")
 under every punctuation variant tried, print-each included; the EBNF's
 own print_stmt production has no otherwise arm either, so the compiler
 is being consistent with its own grammar even though the prose
 describes otherwise as available to both. Print's chain therefore never
 carries one; append's always does, which is how "with and without a
 trailing otherwise" both get covered without emitting the illegal form.
 Hand-verified safe as a block's mandatory closer: unlike For-each/
 While/Repeat, a but-if chain's own trailing period does not leave
 anything open behind it (a for-each-in-while sole-body probe traps the
 next statement two-for-one; the equivalent but-if-chain probe does
 not), so both land in 'gen any leaf's pool, no remap needed.)
To 'gen leaf butif print' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'the base value' is 'gen expr'.
    a text called 'the chain' is "Print {'the base value'}".
    a number called 'the branch count' is 'rng below' of 3 add 1.
    a number called 'the branch index' is 1.
    While 'the branch index' is less than or equal to 'the branch count',
        a text called 'the condition' is 'gen condition',
        a text called 'the alternative' is 'gen expr',
        Set 'the chain' to "{'the chain'}, but if {'the condition'} print {'the alternative'}",
        Set 'the branch index' to 'the branch index' add 1.
    a list called 'the lines' is ['the chain'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen leaf butif append' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_lists to gen_lists add 1.
    a text called decl is "a list called l{gen_lists} is []".
    a text called 'the base value' is 'gen append expr'.
    a text called 'the chain' is "append {'the base value'} to l{gen_lists}".
    a number called 'the branch count' is 'rng below' of 3 add 1.
    a number called 'the branch index' is 1.
    While 'the branch index' is less than or equal to 'the branch count',
        a text called 'the condition' is 'gen condition',
        a text called 'the alternative' is 'gen append expr',
        Set 'the chain' to "{'the chain'}, but if {'the condition'} append {'the alternative'}",
        Set 'the branch index' to 'the branch index' add 1.
    a text called 'the otherwise value' is 'gen append expr'.
    Set 'the chain' to "{'the chain'}, otherwise append {'the otherwise value'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'the chain'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(`treating X as Y`: per-clause substitution on loop expansion. The
 print-each variant sometimes draws its collection from arguments's all
 (LANGUAGE.md's own canonical example). The brief's own item 5 flags
 that arguments's positional properties (first/second/last) need real
 arguments passed to be tested meaningfully, which this generator does
 not have - but arguments's all does not need that: it is a legal,
 always-empty collection with no arguments passed, so the treating
 clause parses and runs zero times, the same defined "does nothing"
 behaviour LANGUAGE.md documents for any empty collection.
 The grid variant chains two `each` clauses, each with its own treating,
 through f3 (language note 3's zero/one/two-arg prelude), reusing the
 grid's own e{N} loop-variable counter so two grid calls in one program
 never collide. Hand-verified UNSAFE as a block's mandatory closer -
 loop expansion is a nesting clause exactly like For-each's own key/
 element iteration (kinds 9/14) and the grid's `each ... and each ...`
 (kind 6's grid quarter) - so both remap to 1 under avoid-onerror and
 stay out of 'gen any leaf', same rule, same reason.)
To 'gen leaf treating print' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'source choice' is 'rng below' of 2.
    a text called source is "".
    a text called 'match value' is "".
    a text called 'replacement value' is "".
    If 'source choice' is 0 then,
        (arguments's all yields text elements (argv strings) - the
         treating match/replacement must be text too, or nasm rejects
         the emitted assembly with 'symbol _str_eq not defined'
         (hand-reduced: `print each item from arguments's all treating
         98 as 31.` alone reproduces it) - a number literal here compiles
         past the analyzer but the codegen path for a text-typed
         collection expects a text-typed match, and the mismatch never
         gets caught until the assembler.)
        Set source to "arguments's all",
        Set 'match value' to "\"-\"",
        Set 'replacement value' to "\"none\"".
    If 'source choice' is 1 then,
        a number called 'elem one' is 'rng below' of 100,
        a number called 'elem two' is 'rng below' of 100,
        a number called 'match number' is 'rng below' of 100,
        a number called 'replacement number' is 'rng below' of 100,
        Set source to "[{'elem one'}, {'elem two'}]",
        Set 'match value' to "{'match number'}",
        Set 'replacement value' to "{'replacement number'}".
    a text called line is "print each item from {source} treating {'match value'} as {'replacement value'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [line].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen leaf treating grid' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'first match' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'first replacement' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'second match' is 'rng below' of 5 add 1.
    a number called 'second replacement' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'elem one' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'elem two' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'range start' is 'rng below' of 5 add 1.
    a number called 'range spread' is 'rng below' of 4.
    a number called 'range end' is 'range start' add 'range spread'.
    Set gen_grid to gen_grid add 1.
    a number called 'first loop name' is gen_grid.
    Set gen_grid to gen_grid add 1.
    a number called 'second loop name' is gen_grid.
    a text called line is "f3 of each e{'first loop name'} from [{'elem one'}, {'elem two'}] treating {'first match'} as {'first replacement'} and each e{'second loop name'} from {'range start'} to {'range end'} treating {'second match'} as {'second replacement'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [line].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(`While` and `Repeat`: today only `For each` is emitted. Both are
 self-contained, single-statement leaves with a hardcoded, unconditional
 `Increment`, never a body drawn from the recursive statement generator.

 The real defect this sidesteps. An earlier draft of this comment
 misdiagnosed it as While-specific; direct reproduction traced it to
 its actual root and filed it as bug #27: **a period never closes a
 Repeat body, in any nesting, at
 any depth - only a blank line does.** `Repeat 2 times, Print "r".`
 followed by `Print "after".`, with no blank line between them, prints
 `r after r after` - "after" is silently absorbed into the loop and
 re-runs with it, not a parse error, not a crash, just a program that
 means something other than what it reads as. `While` and `For each`
 both close correctly on a period, including a period stacked to close
 two levels at once (a For-each nested inside a While, closed with two
 periods on its own last statement, correctly closes both and the
 following statement runs once) - hand-verified, and it is what
 isolates the defect to Repeat specifically rather than "loops in
 general" or "While as the outer level," both of which an earlier
 version of this comment wrongly concluded from a Repeat-nested-in-
 While probe whose double-period failure was actually bug #27 wearing
 a While-shaped disguise.

 Consequence for this generator: 'gen leaf repeat' cannot just emit its
 closing `Print` as the next period-terminated line the way 'gen leaf
 while' does - that line would silently become part of the Repeat's own
 body (verified: it printed the counter once per iteration instead of
 once after, exactly bug #27's signature). It emits a blank line after
 the Repeat statement instead, which is what LANGUAGE.md documents as
 the only thing that closes a Repeat, before the closing `Print`.
 'gen leaf while' needs no such treatment - its own closing `Print`
 already closes correctly on its ordinary period, per the hand-
 verification above, so it is written the simpler way.

 Both stay reachable only at the true top level regardless (kind range
 widened at depth 3 only, left out of depth 2/1/0's ranges and out of
 'gen any leaf'): a blank line closes every open clause at once,
 including an enclosing function definition, so emitting one from
 inside a nested body would be unsafe for reasons that have nothing to
 do with bug #27, and 'gen leaf while' has no need to be reachable
 there either, so both are held to the same, simplest-to-verify
 position. Top level is also where 'nested' is always false, so every
 rendered line is independently period-closed to begin with - the
 blank line in 'gen leaf repeat' is closing the Repeat, not doing any
 work a period was already doing.)
To 'gen leaf while' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_whiles to gen_whiles add 1.
    a number called 'the limit' is 'rng below' of 8 add 1.
    a text called decl is "a number called u{gen_whiles} is 0".
    a text called 'loop line' is "While u{gen_whiles} is less than {'the limit'}, Increment u{gen_whiles}".
    a text called 'read line' is "Print u{gen_whiles}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'loop line', 'read line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Bug #27 (see above): the Repeat statement's own period never closes
 it, so the closing `Print` is emitted after a blank line - the one
 thing that does close a Repeat - instead of joined onto 'the lines'
 the way every other leaf's closing read is.)
To 'gen leaf repeat' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_repeats to gen_repeats add 1.
    a number called 'the trips' is 'rng below' of 8 add 1.
    a text called decl is "a number called r{gen_repeats} is 0".
    a text called 'loop line' is "Repeat {'the trips'} times, Increment r{gen_repeats}".
    a list called 'the opening lines' is [decl, 'loop line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the opening lines'.
    'gen emit' of "\n".
    a text called 'read line' is "Print r{gen_repeats}".
    a list called 'the closing lines' is ['read line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the closing lines'.

(`environment's`: bug #24 (an unset variable segfaulted, uncatchable by
 On error) and bug #26 (out-of-range positional properties - first/last
 on an empty environment) - both fixed in 0.4.3/0.4.4 per CHANGELOG, so
 both are now defined-behaviour-under-test, the same standing the
 buffer/list/map oob leaves already have.

 The inrange leaf reads real environment state - count, a named lookup
 of PATH - but two fixed regressions mean it must never print the raw
 result, and must never reuse a fixed local name. An earlier version
 printed environment's count and environment's "PATH" directly, and
 one landed in a checked-in test fixture verbatim: home directory, tool
 versions, every installed plugin, on a public repo. Reading is exactly
 the coverage bugs #24 and #26 are about; printing what was read is not
 required to exercise that and is the part that leaked. So every read
 here is immediately collapsed to a boolean before it is printed -
 `as a boolean` (0 is false, nonzero is true) for the count, and a
 buffer's own `empty` property (declaring the buffer directly from
 environment's "PATH", the same construction 'needle buf' elsewhere in
 this file uses to turn a text expression into a buffer) for the named
 lookup, never the text itself. `environment's empty` is already a
 boolean and was never the problem.

 The second regression a fixed local name caused: this leaf is reachable
 both as a nested block's mandatory closer (inside a conditionally-taken
 If branch) and as an ordinary top-level statement, in the same program,
 and a fixed name (`envpathbuf`) collided with itself across the two.
 Hand-reduced to a compiler defect with nothing environment-specific
 left in it: `If false-condition then, a buffer called b is "x".` /
 `a buffer called b is "y".` segfaults even though the first
 declaration's branch never runs - a text/buffer declared on an untaken
 If path corrupts a later, same-named declaration elsewhere. This reads
 like bug #25's family (a declaration on a conditional path leaving bad
 storage behind) but #25's own fix, read literally, only names On
 error/While/for-each/Repeat bodies - not If. Reported separately as a
 finding, not tuned around; the generator-side fix is what every other
 leaf in this file already does and this one alone was not doing -
 counter-suffixed names (env{gen_envs}count, env{gen_envs}pathbuf), so
 two calls can never collide regardless of which one lands inside a
 branch that turns out not to run.

 All five lines are plain reads/declarations with no error path, so this
 stays a safe closer.)
To 'gen leaf environment inrange' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_envs to gen_envs add 1.
    a text called 'count decl' is "a number called env{gen_envs}count is environment's count".
    a text called 'count check' is "Print env{gen_envs}count as a boolean".
    a text called 'empty line' is "Print environment's empty".
    a text called 'path decl' is "a buffer called env{gen_envs}pathbuf is environment's \"PATH\"".
    a text called 'path check' is "Print env{gen_envs}pathbuf's empty".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['count decl', 'count check', 'empty line', 'path decl', 'path check'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(The oob leaf's own local, 'the missing value', is a fixed quoted name
 rather than a counter-suffixed one - safe unlike the inrange leaf's
 former envpathbuf, because this leaf is unsafe-closer/remap-only (see
 below), which this file's own avoid-onerror machinery guarantees means
 its real body only ever renders at the true top level, never inside a
 conditionally-taken branch - there is no second scope for the same
 name to collide with. Counter-suffixing it anyway would cost nothing
 and remove the need to reason about that guarantee holding forever, so
 it is done here too, purely as defensive consistency with the fix
 above rather than because this leaf reproduces the defect.)
To 'gen leaf environment oob' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_envs to gen_envs add 1.
    a number called 'probe id' is 'rng below' of 1000000.
    a text called decl is "a text called 'the missing value {gen_envs}' is environment's \"VOXFUZZ_UNSET_{'probe id'}\"".
    a text called handler is "On error print \"environment variable not set\"".
    a text called 'read line' is "Print 'the missing value {gen_envs}'".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, handler, 'read line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(`arguments's`: bugs #23 and #26 lived here, both fixed. The generated
 program runs with no user arguments (runner.vox's 'run program' always
 passes an empty argument list), so count/name/empty are the properties
 that stay meaningful either way - count is always 1, name is always
 the program's own path, empty is always true, and all three are plain
 reads with no error path, so this is a safe closer. `first`/`second`/
 `last`/`the argument at` would, with zero arguments passed, ALWAYS hit
 their out-of-range path rather than sometimes exercising the in-range
 one - the brief's own instruction is to flag that rather than emit
 something that can only ever prove one thing; noted in the task report
 instead. `arguments's all` still gets exercised for real, just via
 'gen leaf treating print' above, since a treating clause needs a
 collection anyway and an always-empty one is exactly the collection
 arguments's all always is - one fewer construct duplicated as its own
 leaf.)
