(Excludes the three On-error kinds (3, 5, 10). Adjudicated 2026-08-18:
 this is not a parser bug but the termination rule working as
 documented - a period after an On-error handler closes only the
 HANDLER, because On error is itself a nesting clause (LANGUAGE.md rule
 1), so a block whose closer ends on a handler is never actually closed
 by it. Restricting the mandatory closer to non-On-error kinds is
 therefore not a workaround but correctness: the closer's period must
 close the block, and only a plain leaf's period does. The hand-reduced
 evidence lives in docs/findings/parser-reject/1/, which also led to
 vox bug #25. On-error groups remain freely available in non-closing
 positions.

 Also excludes 9's key iteration and 14's list iteration, and swaps 6
 (call) for 19 (the same three call shapes minus the grid variant) -
 same rule 1 as above, not a separate defect. `For each`/grid `each` is
 itself a nesting clause, so when one of these is the mandatory closer,
 its own period closes THAT clause, not the enclosing block the closer
 was supposed to close - note 6b's guarantee ("the closing leaf's
 period closes the block") only holds for a leaf that does not itself
 open a clause. 9 and 14 have no such-clause-free sibling so they are
 dropped outright; 6 does (19), so a call stays available here at only
 the cost of its grid quarter. Full coverage, grid included, remains
 available in every non-closing position - both here and in
 'gen statement's own remap (which this mirrors, for the same reason:
 that path picks leaves directly too, with no second call through this
 function, so it needs the identical substitution).

 T3 additions 20-24 (but-if print/append, environment/arguments inrange,
 deep arithmetic) join this pool - each is hand-verified safe as a sole
 closer, per its own function comment. 25-28 (treating, environment oob,
 rich condition) do not, for the same reasons kind 9/14/6-grid don't;
 29-30 (While/Repeat) aren't reachable outside the true top level at
 all, so they can never even be offered here.)
To 'gen any leaf' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called pick is 'rng below' of 16.
    a number called kind is 0.
    If pick is 0 then,
        Set kind to 0.
    If pick is 1 then,
        Set kind to 1.
    If pick is 2 then,
        Set kind to 2.
    If pick is 3 then,
        Set kind to 4.
    If pick is 4 then,
        Set kind to 7.
    If pick is 5 then,
        Set kind to 8.
    If pick is 6 then,
        Set kind to 11.
    If pick is 7 then,
        Set kind to 12.
    If pick is 8 then,
        Set kind to 13.
    If pick is 9 then,
        Set kind to 15.
    If pick is 10 then,
        Set kind to 19.
    If pick is 11 then,
        Set kind to 20.
    If pick is 12 then,
        Set kind to 21.
    If pick is 13 then,
        Set kind to 22.
    If pick is 14 then,
        Set kind to 23.
    If pick is 15 then,
        Set kind to 24.
    'gen dispatch leaf' of kind and indent and nested.

(A block's body always ends with a plain leaf statement (note 6b): the
 leaf's own period is what closes the block, whether or not the body
 also contains a nested loop or if before it. That closing leaf must
 never be an On-error kind - see the note on 'gen statement's
 'avoid onerror' parameter for why - so this always passes true.)
To 'gen body' with a number called depth and a text called indent.
    a boolean called 'block started' is 'gen statement' of depth and indent and true.
    If 'block started' then,
        'gen any leaf' of indent and true.

To 'gen loop' with a number called depth and a text called indent.
    a number called 'the spread' is 'rng below' of 5.
    a number called 'the iteration limit' is 'the spread' add 1.
    a text called head is "{indent}For each number from 1 to {'the iteration limit'},\n".
    'gen emit' of head.
    a number called 'next depth' is depth minus 1.
    a text called 'next indent' is "{indent}  ".
    'gen body' of 'next depth' and 'next indent'.

(Not a compiler defect, and not something 'gen if' itself needs to work
 around - recorded here only because it explains why 'gen any leaf'
 refuses every For-each-bearing kind the guaranteed-closer role (see
 that function's own comment). LANGUAGE.md rule 1: a period closes only
 the innermost currently open clause. If a `For each` (block or
 single-line) is the body of a single-branch If, the For-each's own
 period closes the FOR EACH, not the If - the If is still open one
 level further out than the indentation suggests. Whatever textually
 "follows" the If at this point is therefore still inside its body, and
 it is THAT statement's period which finally closes the If. A
 declaration positioned there is accordingly "declared in only some
 branches" (there is no Otherwise), so vox correctly refuses it outside
 - not a defect, the documented branch-scoping rule doing exactly what
 it says. This only bites when the mandatory closer (see 'gen body')
 is itself a For-each-bearing leaf, since a plain leaf's period closes
 the If as intended; excluding those kinds from 'gen any leaf' is the
 whole fix, and a bare Otherwise-less If stays legal, generatable Vox.)
To 'gen if' with a number called depth and a text called indent.
    a text called 'the left operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called 'the right operand' is 'gen var ref'.
    a text called head is "{indent}If {'the left operand'} is greater than {'the right operand'} then,\n".
    'gen emit' of head.
    a number called 'next depth' is depth minus 1.
    a text called 'next indent' is "{indent}  ".
    'gen body' of 'next depth' and 'next indent'.

(depth is a precomputed, decrementing nesting budget (language note 3):
 3 at the true top-level statement stream, capped at 0. new-var (kind
 18) only grows the globally-referenceable v-pool at depth 3, the one
 place a declaration is unconditionally, permanently in scope; nested
 declarations inside gen's leaf kinds stay local to their own
 statement instead. Returns whether this call opened an unclosed
 block (loop/if), so 'gen body' knows whether it must still append a
 mandatory closer.

 'avoid onerror': true whenever this call's chosen leaf might become
 the LAST thing that closes an enclosing block (called from 'gen
 body') - a clause (If or For, at any depth, including the true top
 level) that closes on an On-error handler mis-parses the compiler's
 clause-close bookkeeping if the very next statement also touches
 On-error, corrupting everything after it (hand-reduced against
 0.4.2, matching the adjacency documented on 'gen any leaf'). Passing
 false (only 'gen program's true top-level call does) allows the
 three On-error leaf kinds through undisturbed, since a plain
 top-level statement never closes a clause.

 Kind space: 0-15 are leaves (see 'gen dispatch leaf'), 16 is a loop,
 17 is an if, 18 grows the v-pool, 19 is a remap-only target. T3 adds
 20-24 (always-safe leaves), 25-28 (leaves that need the same
 avoid-onerror remap as 3/5/6/9/10/14, for the reasons given on each
 leaf's own comment), and 29-30 (While/Repeat, top-level only - see
 'gen leaf while's comment for why). depth 3 draws from the full range;
 depth 2 and 1 drop new-var (nesting must not permanently grow scope)
 and 29-30 (their termination guarantee depends on being an independent,
 self-closing top-level sentence, which nested positions are not); depth
 0 drops loop/if and every T3 addition too, leaves 0-15 only (nesting
 budget exhausted - kept to the pre-T3 range rather than widened, since
 the deepest level is the lowest-value place to add more surface).)
To 'gen statement' with a number called depth and a text called indent and a boolean called 'avoid onerror'.
    a number called kind is 0.
    If depth is equal to 3 then,
        Set kind to 'rng below' of 31.
    If depth is equal to 2 then,
        Set kind to 'rng below' of 29.
    If depth is equal to 1 then,
        Set kind to 'rng below' of 29.
    If depth is equal to 0 then,
        Set kind to 'rng below' of 16.
    (kind 18 (new-var) is only in the depth-2/1 range's span because 29
     needs a single contiguous rng_below upper bound to cover 20-28 too;
     depth 3's own draw is what makes 18 meaningful, so a depth-2/1 hit
     on it is neutralized here rather than left to grow the permanent
     v-pool from a conditionally-executed nested branch.)
    If kind is 18 and depth is equal to 2 then,
        Set kind to 1.
    If kind is 18 and depth is equal to 1 then,
        Set kind to 1.
    a boolean called 'is nested' is true.
    If depth is equal to 3 then,
        Set 'is nested' to false.
    If kind is 16 then,
        'gen loop' of depth and indent,
        Return a boolean, true.
    If kind is 17 then,
        'gen if' of depth and indent,
        Return a boolean, true.
    If kind is 18 then,
        a number called ignored is 'gen new var',
        Return a boolean, false.
    a number called 'safe kind' is kind.
    (Pre-existing generator defect, found and fixed while extending this
     function: the innermost 'If kind is 10' only closes itself with one
     period (rule 1), so without a second one here the outer 'If avoid
     onerror' clause was never closed and silently absorbed the dispatch
     call and the Return below into its own conditional body. At the
     true top level avoid_onerror is false, so that swallowed tail never
     ran - every plain top-level leaf silently emitted nothing, the
     whole time, for every seed. It went unnoticed because loop/if
     bodies recurse through the separately-closed avoid_onerror=true
     path below 'gen body', which still produced visible leaf output,
     making a program look populated even with the true top level
     silently empty. The double period closes both the inner-if and the
     outer avoid-onerror clause, so the dispatch call actually runs
     unconditionally, as the indentation always implied it should.)
    (Same remap for the three For-each-bearing kinds (6's grid variant,
     9, 14) and the same reason as the On-error trio above: whenever
     this leaf might be the one whose period closes an enclosing block
     (avoid_onerror true), a `For each`/grid `each` clause inside it is
     itself a nesting clause (LANGUAGE.md rule 1), so its period closes
     THAT, not the enclosing block - the enclosing If or loop is then
     one level further open than the rest of this generator assumes,
     and whatever comes next is silently declared inside it instead of
     at the level intended. Not a compiler defect (see 'gen if's own
     comment) - the compiler is right that a declaration only inside a
     single If branch is out of scope after it; the fix is simply never
     letting one of these be the block's closer. 9 and 14 have no safe
     sibling, so they fall back to 1 (assign); 6 does have one - 19 is
     'gen leaf call' minus the grid variant - so a call as closer keeps
     three quarters of its coverage instead of losing all of it.

     Every branch below tests the ORIGINAL kind, never 'safe kind' -
     these are independent substitutions, not a chain. kind 10 (map
     oob) used to hop to 9 (map inrange) as its On-error remap, back
     when 9 was itself a safe closer; now that 9 is unsafe too, 10 has
     to be remapped directly to 1, the same as 9 and 14 - routing it
     through 9 first would only test the untouched original `kind`
     (still 10) against "kind is 9", never fire, and leave 'safe kind'
     sitting at 9, still For-each-bearing. Hand-reduced: this exact
     chaining mistake was live in an earlier version of this remap and
     produced seed 3's "Unknown map: m3" before it was caught.)
    If 'avoid onerror' then,
        If kind is 3 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 2.
        If kind is 5 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 4.
        If kind is 10 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 9 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 14 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 6 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 19.
        If kind is 25 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 26 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 27 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1.
        If kind is 28 then,
            Set 'safe kind' to 1..
    'gen dispatch leaf' of 'safe kind' and indent and 'is nested'.
    Return a boolean, false.

(Prelude: thing types and functions, emitted once per program before
 the statement stream that uses them (definitions before instances,
 instances before access - valid by construction). t3 nests one t1 and
 one t2, so a chained possessive is always reachable. t4 carries the
 manifest for 'gen leaf thing member' - a maker ('made at', first
 parameter is a number, not a t4) and a receiver ('reflected across',
 first parameter is a t4), each obeying the owner-return rule, the same
 shape as LANGUAGE.md's own point/'placed at' example (finding 03).
 f1/f2/f3 cover 0, 1, and 2 number parameters.)
To 'gen emit prelude things'.
    'gen emit' of "A thing called t1 has\n".
    'gen emit' of "    a number called x1 is 0,\n".
    'gen emit' of "    a number called x2 is 0.\n".
    'gen emit' of "\n".
    'gen emit' of "A thing called t2 has\n".
    'gen emit' of "    a number called x1 is 0,\n".
    'gen emit' of "    a number called x2 is 0.\n".
    'gen emit' of "\n".
    'gen emit' of "A thing called t3 has\n".
    'gen emit' of "    a t1 called g1,\n".
    'gen emit' of "    a t2 called g2.\n".
    'gen emit' of "\n".
    'gen emit' of "A thing called t4 has\n".
    'gen emit' of "    a function called 'made at',\n".
    'gen emit' of "    a function called 'reflected across',\n".
    'gen emit' of "    a number called x1 is 0.\n".
    'gen emit' of "\n".

To 'gen emit prelude thing methods'.
    'gen emit' of "To do the t4's 'made at', with a number called x1.\n".
    'gen emit' of "    a t4 called plotted.\n".
    'gen emit' of "    Set plotted's x1 to x1.\n".
    'gen emit' of "    Return a t4, plotted.\n".
    'gen emit' of "\n".
    'gen emit' of "To do the t4's 'reflected across', with a t4 called original.\n".
    'gen emit' of "    a t4 called mirrored.\n".
    'gen emit' of "    Set mirrored's x1 to 0 subtract original's x1.\n".
    'gen emit' of "    Return a t4, mirrored.\n".
    'gen emit' of "\n".

To 'gen emit prelude functions'.
    a number called c1 is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a text called fn1 is "To f1.\n    Return a number, {c1}.\n\n".
    'gen emit' of fn1.
    a number called c2 is 'rng below' of 50 add 1.
    a text called fn2 is "To f2 with a number called p1.\n    a number called r is p1 add {c2}.\n    Return a number, r.\n\n".
    'gen emit' of fn2.
    a text called fn3 is "To f3 with a number called p1 and a number called p2.\n    a number called r is p1 add p2.\n    Return a number, r.\n\n".
    'gen emit' of fn3.

To 'gen program' with a number called seed and a number called budget.
    clear gen_out.
    Set gen_vars to 0.
    Set gen_bufs to 0.
    Set gen_lists to 0.
    Set gen_instances to 0.
    Set gen_args to 0.
    Set gen_maps to 0.
    Set gen_values to 0.
    Set gen_floats to 0.
    Set gen_texts to 0.
    Set gen_grid to 0.
    Set gen_whiles to 0.
    Set gen_repeats to 0.
    Set gen_envs to 0.
    'rng seed' of seed.
    'gen emit prelude things'.
    'gen emit prelude thing methods'.
    'gen emit prelude functions'.
    a number called ignored1 is 'gen new var'.
    a number called ignored2 is 'gen new var'.
    a number called i is 0.
    While i is less than budget,
        a boolean called 'top opened' is 'gen statement' of 3 and "" and false,
        Set i to i add 1.
    a number called code is 'rng below' of 256.
    a text called tail is "Exit {code}.\n".
    'gen emit' of tail.

(256, not 101: the shell-era cap is gone, and codes above 128 are the
 ones a shell-reported status would have misclassified as signal deaths -
 the native harness must prove it tells them apart, so the generator must
 produce them.)

To 'gen text'.
    a text called t is "{gen_out}".
    Return a text, t.

To 'gen write' with a text called path.
    open a file for writing called gf at path.
    Write gen_out to gf.
    Close gf.

(Byte-scan a needle over gen_out - used by tests to assert the
 generator actually produced a given construct.)
To 'gen text matches at' with a number called start and a buffer called needle and a number called 'needle length'.
    a number called offset is 0.
    a boolean called 'bytes match' is true.
    While offset is less than 'needle length',
        a number called 'hay pos' is start add offset,
        a number called 'hay byte' is byte 'hay pos' of gen_out,
        a number called 'needle pos' is offset add 1,
        a number called 'needle byte' is byte 'needle pos' of needle,
        If 'hay byte' is not 'needle byte' then,
            Set 'bytes match' to false.
        Set offset to offset add 1.
    Return a boolean, 'bytes match'.

To 'gen text contains' with a text called needle.
    a buffer called 'needle buf' is "{needle}".
    a number called 'haystack length' is gen_out's length.
    a number called 'needle length' is 'needle buf's length.
    a boolean called found is false.
    a number called 'last start' is 'haystack length' minus 'needle length' add 1.
    a number called 'scan start' is 1.
    While 'scan start' is less than or equal to 'last start',
        a boolean called 'match here' is 'gen text matches at' of 'scan start' and 'needle buf' and 'needle length',
        If 'match here' then,
            Set found to true.
        Set 'scan start' to 'scan start' add 1.
    Return a boolean, found.
